# 🛰️ Nmap — Host Discovery

> **TryHackMe Room:** Nmap  
> **Focus Area:** Network Host Discovery (finding *live* hosts on a network before scanning their ports)

---

## 📌 Overview

Before scanning ports on a target, the first step in any reconnaissance workflow is to figure out **which hosts on a network are actually alive**. Nmap offers multiple host-discovery techniques that rely on different protocols — **ARP, ICMP, TCP, and UDP** — each useful in different network conditions (local vs remote, firewall present vs absent).

This writeup documents every host discovery technique and flag covered, along with the underlying networking concepts (TCP flags, CIDR, subnetting) needed to fully understand *why* each scan works the way it does.

---

## 🔍 The `-sS` Flag (SYN Scan Fundamentals)

```bash
sudo nmap -sS example.com
```

- Sends a **SYN packet** to the target port — this is the first step of the TCP 3-way handshake.
- If the port is **open** → target responds with **SYN/ACK** → Nmap immediately sends an **RST** to tear down the connection before it fully completes.
- If the port is **closed** → target responds directly with **RST**.
- Because the full TCP handshake is never completed, this is called a **half-open scan** — it's fast and generates fewer logs on the target, making it stealthier than a full connect scan.
- Requires **raw socket privileges** (`sudo`/root) to craft the packet manually.

