## 🌐 IP Ranges & Subnetting Concepts

### IP Ranges (Same Subnet, Multiple Hosts)

```bash
nmap 10.11.12.15-20
```
This scans 6 consecutive IPs within the **same local subnet**: `.15, .16, .17, .18, .19, .20` — the range only varies in the last octet, meaning all these hosts belong to the same network segment.

### CIDR Notation (`/xx`) & Subnet Masks

```bash
nmap MACHINE_IP/30
```

CIDR notation represents the **subnet mask** — it tells Nmap how many bits of the 32-bit IPv4 address are reserved for the **network** portion vs the **host** portion.

**Formula:**
```
Total IPs = 2^(32 - CIDR number)
```

| CIDR | Host Bits | Total IPs |
|------|-----------|-----------|
| /30  | 2         | 4         |
| /29  | 3         | 8         |
| /28  | 4         | 16        |
| /27  | 5         | 32        |
| /24  | 8         | 256       |
| /16  | 16        | 65,536    |

### 🏷️ Network Address vs Broadcast Address

Every subnet reserves its **first** and **last** address:

Example: `192.168.1.0/30` → 4 total addresses: `.0, .1, .2, .3`

| Address | Role |
|---------|------|
| `192.168.1.0` (first) | **Network Address** — identifies the subnet itself, not assigned to any device |
| `192.168.1.1` – `192.168.1.2` | **Usable Host Addresses** — can be assigned to actual devices |
| `192.168.1.3` (last) | **Broadcast Address** — a packet sent here reaches *every* device on that subnet simultaneously |

> 📝 Nmap still scans **all 4 addresses** in a `/30` (including network & broadcast), even though only 2 are technically "usable" hosts.

