## 🖧 Host Discovery Techniques (Protocol by Protocol)

| Scan Type | Example Command | How It Works |
|-----------|------------------|--------------|
| **ARP Scan** | `sudo nmap -PR -sn 10.200.6.0/24` | Sends a Layer 2 broadcast ARP request ("Who has this IP?"). Only works on the **local network/broadcast domain**. Fast & reliable — the default & best method for LAN discovery. |
| **ICMP Echo Scan** | `sudo nmap -PE -sn 10.200.6.0/24` | This is literally what a normal `ping` does — sends an **ICMP Echo Request**, expects an **ICMP Echo Reply**. Any reply = host is alive. |
| **ICMP Timestamp Scan** | `sudo nmap -PP -sn 10.200.6.0/24` | Used as a fallback when Echo (ping) is blocked by a firewall. Asks the target "what's your current system time?" — any reply confirms the host is alive (the actual time value doesn't matter to Nmap). |
| **ICMP Address Mask Scan** | `sudo nmap -PM -sn 10.200.6.0/24` | Another Echo fallback — asks the target for its **subnet mask**. A reply (regardless of the mask value) confirms the host is up. Mostly legacy — modern OSes rarely respond to this. |
| **TCP SYN Ping Scan** | `sudo nmap -PS22,80,443 -sn 10.200.6.0/30` | Sends a SYN packet to specific ports just for **discovery**, not port-state analysis. Whether the reply is SYN/ACK (open) or RST (closed), *either* response confirms the host is alive. Useful when ICMP is blocked but common ports (80, 443, 22) are not. |
| **TCP ACK Ping Scan** | `sudo nmap -PA22,80,443 -sn 10.200.6.0/30` | Sends an ACK packet as if a connection already exists. The target, confused, replies with RST — confirming it's alive. Used to bypass **stateless firewalls** that block SYN but allow ACK packets (assuming they belong to an "established" connection). |
| **UDP Ping Scan** | `sudo nmap -PU53,161,162 -sn 10.200.6.0/30` | Sends a UDP datagram to specific ports (DNS-53, SNMP-161/162). A closed port typically triggers an **ICMP "Port Unreachable"** error — confirming the host is alive. |

> ⚠️ **Key takeaway:** Any response from a host — regardless of protocol — indicates that it is online. That's the entire principle behind host discovery.

